WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.899
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday. Quick
disclaimer, do NOT try this at home. So I

00:00:03.899 --> 00:00:07.680
woke up around 8:30 in downtown LA to meet publicist extraordinaire Elyse Marshall and

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:08.970
best-selling author / old pal

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:12.580
Ransom Riggs who took us to the
building where they filmed part of Blade

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:16.059
Runner which featured a carbonite encased Charlie Chaplin and then we went to

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:20.150
an extremely creepy long abandoned
hospital now sometimes used as a setting

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:23.700
for horror movies. This is the kind of
place Ransom takes his friends, which

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:27.600
won't surprise you if you've read his novel Miss Peregrine's Home for Peculiar Children".

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.740
We visited the eerie chapel which
reminded me of my Chaplin days,

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:36.520
a kitchen where patients meals were
cooked, "Oh, God!", "don't don't don't don't don't

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:38.480
don't don't"  "it's the oldest coffee in the world." "don't tell me what's in there

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.300
don't tell me what's in there" "just look" "no!" "oh, it's amazing,

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:47.480
it's kind of like Bill Nye" "alright, I'm gonna let the camera look but I'm not gonna look myself."

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:52.020
"Get a little closer. Yeah, there you go." And
there was an endless string of hospital

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.660
rooms caked with mildew

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:57.920
and bird dung. So many of the windows were
blown out that it was hard to say for sure

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.900
whether the place was inside or outside. And I could hear birds singing

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:06.420
and rodents scurrying in the ceilings.
Like many abandoned buildings, there was

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:07.960
evidence of recent squatters,

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:11.720
poorly spelled graffiti, nasty nasty
water fountains,

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:15.920
but what struck me most were the
skeletal remains of dozens of hospital beds.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.260
And then we made our way down to the basement, finding the morgue.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.900
By this time even Ransom, who is hard to scare,

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:26.200
was getting pretty creeped out. "Jesus!"

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:28.720
[John laughing]. I of course am very

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:33.200
easy to scare, Hank. In an operating room, much of the equipment still there,

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.340
miraculously unlooted,

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:39.600
I saw a figure in the mirror, "oh my god!",
and about peed myself before realizing

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:40.360
it was me.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:44.140
We then found our way to the records
room, full of EKGs of hearts that had stopped

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:45.580
beating decades ago.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:50.060
Finally, we made our way to the boiler
room - the beautiful dead guts of the hospital.

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:53.440
Hank,  abandoned building scare the crap
out of me, but I love

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:57.380
tunnels. Steam tunnels, storm drains,
sewers, whatever.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:00.020
but the sewer entrance looked a smidge
unappealing

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:04.840
so instead we fumbled around until we
found the steam tunnels.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:08.180
"yeah, we're well underground now."

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:11.740
as we continued, it got colder. The walls
narrowed.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:14.880
"Can you give me the light? Does that end?" [shuffling]

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:20.680
"Uh, it's hard to tell. I should've brought my police flashlight." "Alright, I'm gonna go."

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.520
"Okay. That was gross. Don't trip."

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:27.700
That tunnel lead to a flooded
room and then to another tunnel

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:30.780
and then to a huge room, entirely empty

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:35.880
except for this bloody bathtub. Horror movie
leftover - I hope!

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:39.220
By than I'd had enough, and as we
made our way up and out of the hospital's

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:42.280
bowels, I found myself wondering what the
hell I was doing.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:47.460
It's true that such spaces remind us, in
a scary but seductive way, of our own temporariness

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:51.000
and the relatively short life of all
that we build and do,

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.680
and it's true that the abject darkness
of the tunnels

00:02:54.680 --> 00:03:00.000
make the world outside impossibly bright and alive, but this is also true: it is nice,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.390
on a Thursday morning in April, to be a
kid for a while,

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:08.159
exploring with old friends, never knowing
what wonder or terror may await

00:03:08.159 --> 00:03:12.659
around the corner. And isn't that why we
explore and also why we read and watch

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:15.800
sports and browse Tumblr and study
astrophysics?

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:19.409
whether we're watching horror movies or
accidentally visiting their sets,

00:03:19.409 --> 00:03:23.720
I think we're after the terrifying,
awesome, otherworldly feeling

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:27.980
of not knowing what lies in wait. Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

